  "shorts": [
    {
      "short_id": "short265",
      "angle": "Two utility accounts differed by one middle initial, while the record itself disagreed about how the second account arose.",
      "avoids": "No claim of overbilling; no unqualified adoption of the record's conflicting account; no claim that a hearing prevents all shutoffs.",
      "funnel_question_left_for_longform": "If the record cannot settle which account is yours, who can resolve the dispute before shutoff?",
      "loop": true,
      "loop_join": "The last line returns to the one-letter difference between the two names.",
      "era": "Memphis, Tennessee, 1970s",
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "L1",
          "text": "A Memphis household began receiving two utility bills. One named Willie S. Craft. The second account named Willie C. Craft: one middle initial apart.",
          "delivery": "intense",
          "source_lines": [
            "Then, in 1973, the Crafts began receiving two bills: their regular bill, and a second bill with an account number in the name of Willie C. Craft, as opposed to Willie S. Craft."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L2",
          "text": "The house had two gas and electric meter sets but only one water meter because the residence had previously been a duplex.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "The residence had been used previously as a duplex."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L3",
          "text": "That physical setup explains why two accounts could exist. It does not prove which charges were right or who caused the accounts to remain separate.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Nobody ever decided whether the Crafts owed the money."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L4",
          "text": "The opinion's record points in conflicting directions about responsibility, so this Short does not blame the family, a contractor, or the utility.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Three courts, four years, and a record that never agreed with itself — all out of what happened at the other end of that line."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L5",
          "text": "Mary Craft repeatedly tried to get an explanation after a final notice and told the office that she had paid a bill.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "She was given no satisfaction."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L6",
          "text": "The Court's account says service was terminated five times for nonpayment. That count is attributed to the Court, not presented as an undisputed fact.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "During this period, the Crafts' utility service was terminated five times for nonpayment."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L7",
          "text": "And no court in this case decided whether the money on the disputed account was actually owed.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Not the trial court. Not the court of appeals. Not the Supreme Court."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L8",
          "text": "The legal fight therefore grew from a record that could not settle its own starting point: Willie C. and Willie S., one letter apart.",
          "delivery": "calm",
          "source_lines": [
            "Nobody ever decided whether the Crafts owed the money."
          ],
          "claims": []
        }
      ],
