    {
      "short_id": "short267",
      "angle": "The dissent described an operating phone system for about two thousand monthly cutoffs, while the majority preserved shutoff after a hearing.",
      "avoids": "No claim of overbilling; no unqualified adoption of the record's conflicting account; no claim that a hearing prevents all shutoffs.",
      "funnel_question_left_for_longform": "What did the Court leave undecided, and did the Crafts ever recover any money?",
      "loop": true,
      "loop_join": "The last line returns from the surviving shutoff power to the monthly cutoff system in the hook.",
      "era": "Memphis, Tennessee, 1970s",
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "L1",
          "text": "The utility terminated service for about two thousand customers each month. That number appears in the dissent's description of how the existing system worked.",
          "delivery": "intense",
          "source_lines": [
            "Each month the Division terminates the service of about 2,000 customers."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L2",
          "text": "Its notices printed an information-center phone number answered by thirty or forty employees who could delay cutoffs for three days.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "These employees also direct callers to credit counselors who are authorized to resolve disputes on a more permanent basis and who can set up extended payment plans for customers in financial difficulty."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L3",
          "text": "Those employees could also refer callers to credit counselors with broader authority to resolve disputes and arrange extended payment plans.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Calls to the listed phone number are answered by 30 or 40 Division employees, all of whom are empowered to delay cutoffs for three days based on representations made by customers over the phone."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L4",
          "text": "The dissent believed that machinery was enough and accused the majority of confusing and trivializing the constitutional principle at stake.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Justice Stevens, dissenting, described the same operation with numbers the majority never uses."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L5",
          "text": "The majority disagreed because the notice itself did not clearly tell customers how and where to obtain a meaningful hearing.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Pay or face termination."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L6",
          "text": "But winning a right to notice and a hearing did not erase the utility's power to terminate service.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Retain the option to terminate."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L7",
          "text": "After providing that opportunity and deciding the amount was justly due, the utility still retained the option to cut service.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "After affording the opportunity. After concluding the amount was justly due."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L8",
          "text": "So the case added procedure before shutoff, not immunity from shutoff, returning to the monthly system that began this dispute.",
          "delivery": "calm",
          "source_lines": [
            "Then the meter reader. About five days after that, everything else."
          ],
          "claims": []
        }
      ],
